<%#
  Pagination, rendered in the table card's footer slot:

    render "shared/essentials/card", padded: false,
           footer: essentials_pagination_footer(@paginated_things) do

  It renders for any table that has rows, including one that fits on a single page: the count
  is the point, and a card that gains and loses a footer depending on how much data happens to
  be in it is a card of no fixed shape.

  It draws no border and no padding of its own -- the card footer already supplies
  `border-t border-slate-200 px-5 py-3`. It used to draw both, which put the pager inside two
  stacked hairlines twelve pixels apart on every page that used the footer slot.

  A table that is not the card's last child -- a tab panel, say -- supplies that chrome itself,
  by assigning essentials_pagination_footer to a local and wrapping it in the same div.

  Go through the helper either way. It returns nil for a collection with no rows at all, which
  is what keeps the card from drawing an empty strip under an empty state.

  The wrapper is a div, not a nav: kaminari's own paginator emits the nav landmark labelled
  "Pagination", and nesting one inside another gives a screen-reader user two identically
  named landmarks for the same control.
%>
<% if collection.respond_to?(:total_pages) && collection.total_count.positive? %>
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3">
    <%# data-pagination-summary is read by auto_submit_controller when a filter applies: it is
        the only sentence on a paginated page that states the total rather than the page. %>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600" data-pagination-summary>
      <%= essentials_pagination_summary(collection) %>
    </p>

    <%#
      Kaminari's Paginator#render evaluates its template only when there is more than one page
      and otherwise returns nil, precisely so the call site can supply fall-back HTML. That is
      the hook used here: a one-page table gets the same three controls in the same places,
      all inert. First and Last are left out, because on a single page they do not name
      anything -- see docs/design-decisions.md.
    %>
    <% if collection.total_pages > 1 %>
      <%= paginate collection, params: (defined?(page_params) ? page_params : {}) %>
    <% else %>
      <nav aria-label="Pagination">
        <ul class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-1">
          <li><span class="pagination-link" aria-disabled="true"><%= raw(t("views.pagination.previous")) %></span></li>
          <li><span class="pagination-link" aria-current="page">1</span></li>
          <li><span class="pagination-link" aria-disabled="true"><%= raw(t("views.pagination.next")) %></span></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
